      <para>In questions 1 – 3, find the average rate of change for the following intervals. Be prepared to show your reasoning.
</para>
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>between noon and 1 p.m.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para/>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>1°F per hour. The temperature rose by 1°F in 1 hour.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>between noon and 4 p.m.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para/>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>1.75°F per hour. The temperature increased by 7°F in 4 hours. The average increase is \(\frac74\) or 1.75.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>between noon and midnight</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para/>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>–0.92°F an hour. I found the slope of the line connecting the two points, and it is \(-\frac{11}{12}\).</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <para>In questions 4 – 6, use the slope formula to find the slope of the line that connects the following points.
</para>
      <!--Q4-->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>\((0, 18)\) and \((1, 19)\)</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para/>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>\(\frac{19-18}{1-0} = 1\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Q5-->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>\((0, 18)\) and \((4, 25)\)</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para/>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>\(\frac{25-18}{4-0} = \frac74\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Q6-->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>\((0, 18)\) and \((12, 7)\)</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para/>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>\(\frac{7-18}{12-0} = \frac{-11}{12}\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Q7-->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>Compare your answers to questions 1 – 3 with your answers to questions 4 – 6. Explain what this tells you about the average rate of change and the slope of a line.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para/>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>The average rate of change between two points is the same as the slope of the line connecting the 2 points.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <para>For questions 8 and 9, think back to Mai and Tyler’s disagreement about temperature to solve these problems.
</para>
      <!--Q8-->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>Use the <term id="term-00001">average rate of change</term> to show which time period—4 p.m. to 6 p.m. or 6 p.m. to 10 p.m.—experienced a faster temperature drop.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para/>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>The temperature dropped faster between 4 p.m. and 6 p.m. For example: In the first interval, the temperature fell 8°F in 2 hours, which is an average drop of 4°F an hour. In the second, it fell 9°F in 4 hours, which is an average drop of 2.25°F per hour.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Q9-->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>Use the slope formula to show which line has the greater slope—the line connecting the points that represent the temperature at 4 p.m. and 6 p.m. or the line connecting the points that represent the temperature at 6 p.m. and 10 p.m.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para/>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>The slope of the line representing the temperature between 4 p.m. and 6 p.m. has a greater slope. The slope of the line connecting the points representing the temperature between 4 p.m. and 6 p.m. is
      \(\frac{17-25}{6-4} = -4\). The slope of the line connecting the points representing the temperature between 6 p.m. and 10 p.m. is \(\frac{8-17}{10-6} = \frac{9}{4}=-2.25\).</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Q10-->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>Compare your answers to question 8 and question 9.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para/>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>Finding the average rate of change and using the slope formula give the same results. The average rate of change and the slope of the line between hours 4 and 6 is greater than the average rate of change and the slope of the line between hours 6 and 10.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!-- "Start "Are you Ready for More? click to reveal.  If you have more than one on a page, you will need to change the Data-fire/data-wait-for-events for each set.-->
      
        <exercise>
          <problem>
            
            
          </problem>
        </exercise>
      
      <!--Start interaction.  If multiple interactions appear under "are you ready for more?, they should all have matching "data-wait-for-event", which should also match the "data-fire-event" for the button. Note in this sample, they are all RFM1-->
      <exercise class="RFM1">
        <problem>
          <title>Extending Your Thinking</title>
          <para>Refer to graph and table of the previous function \(T\) to answer the questions:</para>
          <para> Over what interval did the temperature decrease the most rapidly?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>between 5 p.m. and 6 p.m.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--End interaction-->
      <exercise class="RFM1">
        <problem>
          <para>Over what interval did the temperature increase the most rapidly?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para/>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>between 3 p.m. and 4 p.m.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--End interaction-->
    </section>

  <note class="self-check"><para>The temperature \(t\), in degrees Fahrenheit, of a cup of coffee that was poured and then forgotten for \(m\) minutes is represented in the table below</para>
    <table class="os-raise-horizontaltable">
      <tgroup cols="5"><tbody><row>
          <entry><emphasis>Time in minutes</emphasis></entry><entry>
            0
          </entry>
          <entry>
